      "thesis": "Micron reported FQ3 (ended 28-May) after the close on 24 June and it was the print of the quarter: record revenue $41.46B (beating ~$35.25B consensus), non-GAAP EPS $25.11 (vs ~$20.28), and a Q4 guide of ~$50B ±$1B (~86% gross margin), with 16 strategic customer agreements (~$22B of deposits/commitments) underpinning it. HBM3E/HBM4 are sold out through calendar 2027 with demand into 2028. The stock gapped to an all-time high near $1,213 on 25 June, then FADED to ~$938 by 7 July as the whole memory complex (MU/Samsung/SK Hynix) dropped >20% into an official bear market on a sell-the-record reflex. That fade has now REVERSED: SK Hynix's US listing debuted on the Nasdaq on 10 July — opening near $170 for a ~14% pop and raising ~$26.5bn, the largest-ever US share sale by a foreign company — and the institutional endorsement lit the group back up, with MU up ~4% on Thursday (SK Hynix's book oversubscribed) on top of a fresh ~$3bn US semiconductor supply-chain investment (including GlobalWafers' Texas wafer plant), lifting it briefly. The pricing supercycle is CONFIRMED — The Transcript's 30-Jun read flags DRAM revenue +343% YoY on PRICE and shortages 'everlasting' through 2028 — but the tape stayed violently two-way: a ~$1.3T AI-capex selloff hit Monday 13-Jul on hyperscaler-capex-unsustainability fear, before the cool-CPI relief rally (14-Jul) reversed it — semis rebounded (SMH +2.5%) and, crucially, IBM's -25% warning revealed the mirror image of the capex fear: clients are cutting legacy software/mainframe spend to pour capex INTO supply-constrained AI servers, storage and MEMORY, a direct demand signal for MU that Japan's memory names (Kioxia +6.7%, Lasertec +6.9%) traded on overnight. So this stays a 'how do I hold a name that made a new high, gave it back, and keeps chopping' problem, not a 'buy the bounce' — but the demand thesis is now confirmed from two independent directions. For concentrated holders, monetise strength with a collar or overwrite; TSMC's 16-Jul print is the next read-through on the whole AI-hardware chain.",
